Yes — in LSEG Open DACS, the term “mounts” has a very specific technical meaning.

Here’s a clear explanation 👇

🔹 What “mounts” mean in Open DACS

In the DACS entitlement system, a mount represents a connection instance (or “position”) between a user/application and a data source (service).

Each mount is essentially a login session from an application to a DACS-managed service (for example, an RTDS/TREP feed).

DACS keeps track of all these mounts in order to:

Enforce concurrent login limits (per user or per application).

Monitor usage — who is connected, to what, and from where.

Apply entitlements and permissions correctly.

You can think of a mount as:

“A logical instance of a user consuming real-time data from a specific service, tracked for entitlement and auditing purposes.”

🔹 Examples

If the same user logs into two different market data terminals or applications, DACS will count two mounts for that user.

If one application connects to multiple services (e.g., IDN_RDF and ELEKTRON_EDGE), it will have a mount per service.

If you have a Redistributor App that uses Open DACS, each downstream user connection typically results in a separate mount record for entitlement tracking.

🔹 Why mounts matter

Licensing & compliance: Exchanges often restrict how many simultaneous connections (mounts) a firm or user can have.

Performance management: Too many mounts can indicate unclosed sessions or misbehaving applications.

Auditing & reporting: DACS administrators use mount data to generate entitlement and usage reports.

🔹 Related terms

Position — sometimes used interchangeably with mount; a user “occupies a DACS position” when connected.

Site — a logical grouping of DACS components; mounts belong to a particular site.

Mount table — a DACS table showing all active connections (mounts), including user ID, application ID, service, and session state.
